dts: rk3576-armsom-sige5: describe the secure world

Adds the linaro,optee-tz firmware node the tee driver binds to, and
reservations covering the memory a secure world owns.

Confirmed on hardware with Rockchip's blob. The boot reads:

  ## Checking hash(es) for Image tee-1 ... sha256+ OK
  I/TC: OP-TEE version: 3.13.0-1075-g72fc9ecd7, fwver: v1.12
  I/TC: OP-TEE memory size: TEEOS 0x200000 TA 0xc00000 SHM 0x200000
  I/TC: Primary CPU switching to normal world boot

and with the reservation in place Linux logs

  OF: reserved mem: 0x48400000..0x491fffff (14336 KiB) nomap optee@48400000
  optee: revision 3.13, dynamic shared memory is enabled, initialized driver

giving /dev/tee0 and /dev/teepriv0. Before the firmware node existed,
/proc/device-tree/firmware held only scmi and the driver had nothing to bind
to.

Three regions rather than one because the two secure worlds this system can
build sit at different addresses, and one kernel image should boot on either.
Rockchip's blob loads at 0x48400000 and reports 14 MB; upstream OP-TEE uses
TZDRAM 0x70000000 with 32 MB and static shared memory 0x72000000 with 4 MB,
from core/arch/arm/plat-rockchip/conf.mk. Reserving the unused one costs its
size in RAM and nothing else - 50 MB of 8 GB together, cheaper than a kernel
whose reservation has to match the installed bootloader.

All of it is inert in the default build, which ships no BL32: the SMC goes
unanswered so the driver never registers. Without the reservation Linux is
free to allocate over the secure world; it survived one boot here, which is
luck rather than a guarantee.

Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PBGQqVfXFDyZYA6aV3X3Qq

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3576-armsom-sige5.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3576-armsom-sige5.dts
@@ -1314,6 +1314,58 @@
 			#iommu-cells = <0>;
 			power-domains = <&power RK3576_PD_NPU0>,
 					<&power RK3576_PD_NPU1>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * Secure world. Present only when the bootloader carries a BL32; a build
+ * without one leaves the SMC unanswered and the driver does not register,
+ * so these nodes are harmless either way.
+ *
+ * Two regions, because the two secure worlds this system can be built with
+ * sit at different addresses and one kernel image should boot on either:
+ *
+ *   Rockchip's blob (scripts/build-uboot.sh WITH_BL32=1) loads at 0x48400000.
+ *   Its size is published nowhere; OP-TEE reports its own layout at startup,
+ *   "TEEOS 0x200000 TA 0xc00000 SHM 0x200000", which is 14 MB.
+ *
+ *   Upstream OP-TEE (USE_OPENSOURCE_TEE=1) uses CFG_TZDRAM_START 0x70000000
+ *   with 32 MB and CFG_SHMEM_START 0x72000000 with 4 MB, from
+ *   core/arch/arm/plat-rockchip/conf.mk.
+ *
+ * Reserving the unused one costs its size in RAM and nothing else. Both
+ * together are 50 MB of 8 GB, which is cheaper than a kernel whose
+ * reservation has to match the installed bootloader.
+ */
+&{/} {
+	firmware {
+		optee {
+			compatible = "linaro,optee-tz";
+			method = "smc";
 		};
 	};
+
+	reserved-memory {
+		#address-cells = <2>;
+		#size-cells = <2>;
+		ranges;
+
+		/* Rockchip BL32 */
+		optee@48400000 {
+			reg = <0x0 0x48400000 0x0 0xe00000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		/* Upstream OP-TEE: TZDRAM, then its static shared memory */
+		optee@70000000 {
+			reg = <0x0 0x70000000 0x0 0x2000000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		optee@72000000 {
+			reg = <0x0 0x72000000 0x0 0x400000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+	};
 };
